From 82b3985a7cc8bd8b79c2f6f027490dd1f47408f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:24:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09/21] l2r-local: entry 23-a: rt: a per-thread stack of
 pending drops

First step of replacing the retcon destructor executor for
RecordDropKind::Worklist types with an in-place worklist, as Lean's
lean_del frees: free a cell, keep its dead children on a stack, pop the
last first. This patch adds only the runtime part; nothing calls it yet.

Why: the executor keeps one suspended coroutine frame per level of a
value until the level below is freed. A one-sided tree of 10^6 levels
adds 85 bytes per level (left- or right-deep), a 64-arm enum chain whose
recursive member comes first 622 bytes per level (CoroSplit gives each
arm's spilled child its own frame slot). With source-order cleanup that
state per level cannot go away: the parent resumes after its first
child's whole subtree. A worklist whose glue releases a cell's members
at once and defers only its dead children needs no state per level for
any one-sided chain.

drop_stack.rs:
- `run(object, walk)` (C: `__reussir_drop_run`) calls `walk(object)` with
  the thread marked as draining. The outermost run then pops the entries
  that the walk deferred, last deferred first, until none is left; the
  releases it pops may defer more. A run started while the thread drains
  (a drop made by a popped release, or by a foreign hook the drain runs)
  only calls the walk and returns before the dead members it deferred are
  released: the running drain releases them. So the native stack stays
  bounded however deep the value is, for the box edges the glue defers.
- `defer(cell, release)` (C: `__reussir_drop_defer`) pushes one entry
  while the thread drains; outside a drain it runs the release at once,
  as a run of it. The stack never reads or writes `cell`.
- One thread-local without a destructor: the flag and the entries' Vec by
  its raw parts (16 bytes per pending entry). The buffer shrinks back to
  64 entries after a drain; a guard frees it at thread exit, and a drain
  that runs after the guard (a drop in a later thread-local destructor)
  frees the buffer it needed at its end.

Both entry points are registered in symbols.rs for in-process JITs.

Tests (drop_stack/tests.rs): last-deferred-first order with nested
deferrals; a run inside a release joins the drain; a chain of 10^6
cells, each release deferring the next, on a 64 KiB thread stack; 10^4
pending entries, the buffer shrunk afterwards (10^3 and 200 under Miri);
a defer outside a drain runs at once; a drop in a thread-local
destructor after the guard's (no leak under Miri); the two symbols are
exported.
